# Environment / secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# The development TLS material generated by scripts/dev/tls-dev-cert.sh — a local CA, a leaf covering
# the four dev hostnames, and the Traefik dynamic snippet that names them. THE WHOLE DIRECTORY, not
# just the private keys: `*.key` above already covered those, which would have left the CA and leaf
# CERTIFICATES tracked — committing a certificate whose key is deliberately untracked is worse than
# committing neither, because it looks like a shipped trust anchor that nobody can reproduce. Every
# file here is regenerable (`scripts/dev/tls-dev-cert.sh`) and machine-specific: the CA is trusted in
# one operator's OS trust store and means nothing in anyone else's.
infrastructure/docker/traefik/certs/

# Dependencies
node_modules/
vendor/
# pnpm's content-addressable store. NOT the same thing as node_modules: pnpm puts it at the
# repo root whenever HOME is not writable or is redirected into the tree, which is exactly what
# happens when a container runs `pnpm install` with `-e HOME=...` pointing inside the mount.
# Measured at 882 MB–1.3 GB here. Without this line it lands as an untracked directory and a
# `git add -A` stages the better part of a gigabyte of tarballs.
.pnpm-store/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# Build output
dist/
build/
.next/
out/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Test & tooling caches
coverage/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.phpunit.result.cache
storage/phpstan/
playwright-report/
test-results/

# Local data volumes
/data/
*.sqlite
*.db

# Editor / OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.swp

# ================================================================================================
# Infrastructure — appended by platform-devops-engineer
# ================================================================================================

# SBOMs. Generated in the release job and attached to the release, never committed: an SBOM drifts
# from the build the moment a lockfile moves, and a committed one gets quoted as evidence long
# after it stopped describing anything.
sbom/

# Compose secrets. FILES, mounted at /run/secrets/<name> — never environment variables, because
# `docker compose config` renders every interpolated value in full and `docker inspect` shows the
# environment. .gitkeep is kept so the directory exists on a fresh clone; without it the `file:`
# sources resolve to nothing and Docker creates DIRECTORIES at those paths.
infrastructure/docker/secrets/*
!infrastructure/docker/secrets/.gitkeep

# CONTAINER environment (env_file:). The *.env.example templates ARE committed; the rendered *.env
# files are not. Note this is a different directory from the Compose INTERPOLATION file
# (infrastructure/docker/.env), which the generic `.env` rule above already covers.
infrastructure/docker/env/*.env

# Rendered service credential files. Same split as env/ above: the *.example TEMPLATE is committed,
# the rendered file is not, and scripts/dev/bootstrap.sh copies one to the other exactly once and
# then rewrites the placeholders in place. It never copies over an existing file, because that file
# may hold the credential a RUNNING server is authenticating.
#
#   seaweedfs/identities.json  holds a PLAINTEXT S3 secret key, byte-identical to
#                              secrets/s3_secret_key.
#   valkey/users.acl           holds the sha256 ACL password hashes for kb-core, kb-ai and
#                              kb-observer. A hash is not a secret you can use directly, but these
#                              are 24 bytes of base64 behind a single unsalted SHA-256 and the
#                              usernames are published here, so treat them as credential material.
#
# Both were previously COMMITTED, on the stated grounds that an absent file makes SeaweedFS fail
# open and makes Valkey refuse to start. BOTH CLAIMS WERE MEASURED FALSE on 2026-08-11 against the
# pinned tags, and the truth is the reverse of the folklore in each case:
#   chrislusf/seaweedfs:4.40 -- an absent bind source (Docker creates a DIRECTORY) or a zero-byte
#     file is a FATAL startup error, exit 255. It fails CLOSED. What actually fails open is dropping
#     -s3.config from the command, or a config that parses to zero identities.
#   valkey/valkey:9.1.1 -- an absent or zero-byte aclfile does NOT block startup. The server comes
#     up with `user default on nopass ~* &* +@all` and accepts unauthenticated writes. It fails
#     OPEN. The compose healthcheck was extended to catch exactly that; see the comment on it.
# The measurements are recorded in seaweedfs/identities.json.example's __README block.
infrastructure/docker/seaweedfs/identities.json
infrastructure/docker/valkey/users.acl

# Model weights. Gigabytes, absent from every SBOM, invisible to pip-audit, and carrying their own
# licences — Surya's code is Apache-2.0 while its WEIGHTS are OpenRAIL-M. They live on a mounted
# volume and are pinned by commit sha in services/ai-service/models.manifest.toml.
/models/

# Expo / React Native. The native projects are GENERATED by prebuild from app.json and the config
# plugins; committing them makes the generated tree the source of truth and every prebuild a
# conflict.
.expo/
apps/mobile/ios/
apps/mobile/android/

# Playwright. playwright/.auth/ holds real storageState for the test users — cookies and Sanctum
# tokens. It is a live credential store that happens to live in a test directory.
playwright/.auth/
blob-report/

# NOT ignored, deliberately:
#   packages/design-tokens/generated/  — consumed by both apps; a visual change must be visible in
#                                        review, so it is committed and NOT marked linguist-generated
#   infrastructure/docker/seaweedfs/identities.json.example — the committed TEMPLATE. It must stay
#                                        tracked: it is the only thing a fresh clone can render the
#                                        real identities.json from, and preflight compares against
#                                        it to detect a template deployed unchanged.
#   infrastructure/docker/valkey/users.acl.example — likewise. Note it carries NO comment syntax,
#                                        because bootstrap copies it verbatim and `#` starts a
#                                        password hash in an aclfile. See valkey/README.md.
#   infrastructure/docker/seaweedfs/circuit_breaker.json — no credential in it, and it is mounted
#                                        from the same directory, so a missing source would become
#                                        a Docker-created directory and the gateway would run with
#                                        no concurrency or bandwidth limit at all.

# Git bundles are transfer artifacts, never source. One was swept into a commit by an
# `add -A` and caught before it reached the remote; the blob would have been permanent.
*.bundle
